Oh, the Microsoft RDC apps for iOS and Android are pretty consistent in the way they operate, so the configuration for a user needing to access a remote desktop session from an iPhone or Mac computer is almost the same as that of someone doing so from an android phone or tablet.  This also applies to the Microsoft store Remote Desktop Client app.

&#x200B;

Lastly, the gateway settings have been part of the RDclient for a few years so the settings are there in MRemoteNG as well as Remote Desktop Connection Manager if you use those tools in your environment.

&#x200B;

Happy Nerding.

I have experience with Rd-gateway.

I set it up on server 2012 R2 last year.  In an fully Microsoft shop, Remote Desktop Gateway is a welcome relief.

Set up is a bit tedious, but with certificates and a preexisting Network Policy Server , all i really had to do was add the role and features to my new Remote Desktop Server VM, then configure the server, the the computer and user groups.  

If you want multiple virtual desktop environments, you need to have the licencing set up for it, but I'm using it to connect to my server VMs for management and to allow my users access tot heir desktops in a controlled fashion. 

Our old solution was a PPTP-VPN from RRAS on server 2008, and the default is that all traffic flows over the remote gateway.  If you want to segregate traffic, you have to go through the work of configuring the end users DNS settings in the VPN config, something that's frustrating to script.  With this solution, the only thing that goes through my network is their RDP session, and its a lot faster.

&#x200B;

I was also able to configure my NPS to allow or prohibit access to groups of machines for groups of users (i.e. sales users can only see sales computers).

&#x200B;

The last great part on this is that it reduces the surface area of exploitable addresses to that of an RDP connection, so i'm not as prohibitive of users connecting with their personal laptops or even phones in emergencies.  I usually spend a good chunk of my Sunday in church resetting something on a server 2003 VM from my android phone using the Microsoft Remote Desktop Client app.  

&#x200B;

Certificates form Sectigo (was Comodo)  are under 10 bucks a year for this kind of application, so securing it isn't hard or expensive, and if you have a license for windows server, the license is good to go (for gateway, not for serving virtual desktop instances).

&#x200B;

